{"parentUuid":"c18e8d63-dd49-4d67-bd35-8d809745564c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"eb903157-6eb9-4dd8-8710-2e428622c046","agentId":"a6c24b41b58cd6945","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013c6WYTh8VPUwKiCUM28BxT","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Reformulation-Linearization Technique RLT Sherali-Adams hierarchy quadratic 0-1\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"A Reformulation-Linearization Technique (RLT) for semi-infinite and convex programs under mixed 0-1 and general discrete restrictions - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0166218X08002011\"},{\"title\":\"Reformulation–Linearization Techniques for Discrete Optimization Problems | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/rwe/10.1007/978-1-4419-7997-1_45\"},{\"title\":\"Reformulation-Linearization Technique for Global Optimization | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/rwe/10.1007/978-0-387-74759-0_559\"},{\"title\":\"Reformulation‐Linearization Technique for MIPs - Sherali - 2011 - Major Reference Works - Wiley Online Library\",\"url\":\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/9780470400531.eorms0712\"},{\"title\":\"A Reformulation-Linearization Technique for Solving Discrete and Continuous Nonconvex Problems | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4757-4388-3\"},{\"title\":\"A Reformulation-Linearization Technique (RLT) for semi-infinite and convex programs under mixed 0-1 and general discrete restrictions | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/222083578_A_Reformulation-Linearization_Technique_RLT_for_semi-infinite_and_convex_programs_under_mixed_0-1_and_general_discrete_restrictions\"},{\"title\":\"Cutting planes for RLT relaxations of mixed 0–1 polynomial programs | Mathematical Programming | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10107-015-0863-8\"},{\"title\":\"Reformulation–Linearization Techniques for Mixed-Discrete Optimization Problems | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/rwe/10.1007/978-1-4614-6624-6_45-1\"},{\"title\":\"Reformulation-Linearization Technique for Global Optimization | SpringerLink\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/referenceworkentry/10.1007/978-0-387-74759-0_559\"},{\"title\":\"A Reformulation-Linearization Technique for Solving Discrete and Continuous Nonconvex Problems | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/243767084_A_Reformulation-Linearization_Technique_for_Solving_Discrete_and_Continuous_Nonconvex_Problems\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's an overview of the Reformulation-Linearization Technique (RLT) and its connection to the Sherali-Adams hierarchy:\n\n## Reformulation-Linearization Technique (RLT)\n\nThe Reformulation-Linearization Technique (RLT) provides a hierarchy of relaxations spanning the spectrum from the continuous relaxation to the convex hull representation for linear 0-1 mixed-integer and general mixed-discrete programs.\n\n## The RLT Process\n\nThe RLT operates in two phases. In the Reformulation Phase, certain types of additional implied polynomial constraints are appended to the problem. The resulting problem is subsequently linearized in the Linearization/Convexification Phase via the definition of suitable new variables to replace each distinct variable-product term.\n\n## The Sherali-Adams Hierarchy\n\nThe reformulation–linearization technique, due to Sherali and Adams, can be used to construct hierarchies of linear programming relaxations of mixed 0–1 polynomial programs. As one moves up the hierarchy, the relaxations grow stronger, but the number of variables increases exponentially.\n\n## Applications to Quadratic Problems\n\nSherali and Adams developed a hierarchy of relaxations between the continuous and convex hull representations for zero-one programming problems. This framework has been specifically applied to quadratic programming and quadratic assignment problems, among other applications.\n\nThe RLT provides a unified approach for handling both discrete and continuous nonconvex optimization problems, making it a powerful tool in operations research and mathematical optimization.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"01eee905-7b89-4af7-b0a3-2381ab98d557","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:06:28.855Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c18e8d63-dd49-4d67-bd35-8d809745564c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"c93f2470-2c24-4eba-a600-f8aa769da9b1","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"eb903157-6eb9-4dd8-8710-2e428622c046","agentId":"a6c24b41b58cd6945","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SMLd6YWwF8KELzY9pSw2S2","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"OR-Tools CP-SAT add_rlt_cuts parameter linearization\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"ortools.sat.python.cp_model API documentation\",\"url\":\"https://or-tools.github.io/docs/pdoc/ortools/sat/python/cp_model.html\"},{\"title\":\"or-tools/ortools/sat/sat_parameters.proto at stable · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/blob/stable/ortools/sat/sat_parameters.proto\"},{\"title\":\"How does it work? - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/07_under_the_hood.html\"},{\"title\":\"Google AI’s OR-Tools CP-SAT Solver unofficial tutorial Bobak Pezeshki\",\"url\":\"https://ics.uci.edu/~dechter/courses/ics-275/fall-2020/slides/OR-Tools_CP-SAT_Solver_Tutorial.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/\"},{\"title\":\"Basic Modeling - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/04_modelling.html\"}]\n\nThe search results provide some useful context about OR-Tools CP-SAT and linearization concepts, but don't contain specific information about the `add_rlt_cuts` parameter. Let me search more specifically for this parameter.\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"or-tools/ortools/sat/sat_parameters.proto at stable · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/blob/stable/ortools/sat/sat_parameters.proto\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Setting solver limits | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_tasks\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/\"},{\"title\":\"Advanced Modeling - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/04B_advanced_modelling.html\"},{\"title\":\"Google OR-Tools: ortools.math_opt.python.parameters.SolveParameters Class Reference\",\"url\":\"https://or-tools.github.io/docs/python/classortools_1_1math__opt_1_1python_1_1parameters_1_1SolveParameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT for scheduling Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://schedulingseminar.com/presentations/SchedulingSeminar_LaurentPerron.pdf\"}]\n\nGreat! I found the relevant information. Based on the search results, I can now provide you with information about the `add_rlt_cuts` parameter in OR-Tools CP-SAT.\n\n## Search Results Summary\n\nThe `add_rlt_cuts` parameter in CP-SAT is still experimental but can help on binary problems with a lot of clauses of size 3. The parameter has a default value of `true`, meaning RLT (Reformulation Linearization Technique) cuts are enabled by default.\n\nRLT cuts are cutting plane techniques used to strengthen the linear relaxation in the solver's branch-and-cut framework. The parameter allows you to control whether these specialized cuts should be generated during the solving process.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"469f0951-a1c7-402c-9f33-0d13e3eace56","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:06:29.728Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c93f2470-2c24-4eba-a600-f8aa769da9b1","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"fadfb7e8-75f8-4427-9793-1e46b8beca7c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"eb903157-6eb9-4dd8-8710-2e428622c046","agentId":"a6c24b41b58cd6945","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CD3opg27XrLW4J29tcpvXq","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Pyomo Pyomo.GDP RLT reformulation linearization extension McCormick\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Pyomo.GDP: an ecosystem for logic based modeling and optimization development | Optimization and Engineering | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11081-021-09601-7\"},{\"title\":\"Solving Logic-based Models with Pyomo.GDP — Pyomo 6.8.0 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://pyomo.readthedocs.io/en/6.8.0/modeling_extensions/gdp/solving.html\"},{\"title\":\"Generalized Disjunctive Programming — Pyomo 6.8.0 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://pyomo.readthedocs.io/en/6.8.0/modeling_extensions/gdp/index.html\"},{\"title\":\"Pyomo.GDP: an ecosystem for logic based modeling and ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.osti.gov/servlets/purl/1781543\"},{\"title\":\"Solving Logic-based Models with Pyomo.GDP — Pyomo 6.6.2 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://pyomo.readthedocs.io/en/6.6.2/modeling_extensions/gdp/solving.html\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - merlresearch/OptimalRML: Computes the optimal Recursive McCormick Linearizations (RMLs) for Multi Linear Programs (MLPs) · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/merlresearch/OptimalRML\"},{\"title\":\"Pyomo.GDP: Disjunctive Models in Python\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/Papers/Chen_Pyomo_GDP_PSE2018.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"A reformulation-linearization technique for optimization over simplices | Mathematical Programming\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10107-021-01726-y\"},{\"title\":\"Pyomo.GDP: Disjunctive Models in Python - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780444642417501439\"},{\"title\":\"Pyomo.GDP: Disjunctive Models in Python | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/326780000_PyomoGDP_Disjunctive_Models_in_Python\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about Pyomo.GDP and related reformulation techniques:\n\n## Pyomo.GDP Overview\n\nPyomo.GDP provides support for Generalized Disjunctive Programming (GDP), an extension of Disjunctive Programming to include nonlinear relationships.\n\n## Reformulation and Linearization Methods\n\nThe two classical strategies for converting disjunctive models to MILP/MINLP are the Big-M and Hull reformulations. The Big-M reformulation results in a smaller transformed model, avoiding the need to add extra variables; however, it yields a looser continuous relaxation.\n\n### Advanced Reformulations\n\nTrespalacios and Grossmann (2016) introduced a method for strengthening the BM formulation using cutting planes derived from the HR, leading to a hybrid reformulation tighter than the BM, but smaller than the HR.\n\n## McCormick Extensions\n\nThe search results mention McCormick relaxations in the context of Pyomo.GDP's solver implementations. McCormick relaxations (Tsoukalas and Mitsos 2014) are referenced in relation to global optimization approaches used with GDP models.\n\n## Logical Constraint Linearization\n\nPyomo.GDP includes a new logical expression system implementation allowing for a more intuitive description of logical propositions, and the logical expression system supports automated reformulation of these logical constraints to linear constraints.\n\nThe search results provide information about these reformulation techniques, though specific details about an \"RLT reformulation\" extension specifically integrated into Pyomo.GDP are not explicitly detailed in the results.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"657bc561-1c41-4fac-bd91-b0ab4ae04d60","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:06:31.577Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fadfb7e8-75f8-4427-9793-1e46b8beca7c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"9fe8c8ba-e849-441a-93d4-90f9c65e4a82","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"eb903157-6eb9-4dd8-8710-2e428622c046","agentId":"a6c24b41b58cd6945","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016dZMtkyNsLjTtqQpqGztkY","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"RLT lift-and-project 2D placement packing rectangle linearization\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"RLT insights into lift-and-project closures | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/271660283_RLT_insights_into_lift-and-project_closures\"},{\"title\":\"Reformulation–Linearization Techniques for Discrete Optimization Problems | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/rwe/10.1007/978-1-4419-7997-1_45\"},{\"title\":\"RLT insights into lift-and-project closures | Optimization Letters | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11590-014-0763-5\"},{\"title\":\"rectangle-packing-solver · PyPI\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/rectangle-packing-solver/\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - heheda12345/Rectangle-Packing: Project of OOP, Tsinghua University, 2018 spring; Implement of \\\"VLSI Module Placement Based on Rectangle Packing by the Sequence-Pair\\\" · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/heheda12345/Rectangle-Packing\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - kotarot/rectangle-packing-solver: A solver to find a solution of the 2D rectangle packing problem by simulated annealing (SA) optimization. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/kotarot/rectangle-packing-solver\"},{\"title\":\"Mitigating Dimensionality in 2D Rectangle Packing Problem under Reinforcement\",\"url\":\"https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/2409.09677\"},{\"title\":\"[2409.09677] Mitigating Dimensionality in 2D Rectangle Packing Problem under Reinforcement Learning Schema\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.09677\"},{\"title\":\"RLT-POS: Reformulation-Linearization Technique-based optimization software for solving polynomial programming problems | Mathematical Programming Computation | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12532-016-0099-5\"},{\"title\":\"Geometry of the Reformulation-Linearization-Technique: Domination of Disjunctions\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2511.13805\"}]\n\nI found relevant search results for your query about RLT (Reformulation-Linearization Technique), lift-and-project, and related optimization techniques. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Overview of RLT and Lift-and-Project\n\nThe Reformulation-Linearization Technique (RLT) and lift-and-project (L&P) are two methods for constructing polyhedral outer-approximations of mixed 0-1 linear programs, with the latter being obtainable as a relaxed version of the former through the removal of constraints.\n\n## Key Differences\n\nA key difference is that the multipliers used by RLT are defined in terms of the binary variables of the given problem, which, in contrast to those used in L&P, provide important advantages including the ability to handle nonlinear expressions, to recognize redundant inequalities, and to exploit special structures.\n\n## How RLT Works\n\nRLT exploits inherent special structures by identifying particular classes of multiplicative factors that are applied to the original problem to reformulate it as an equivalent polynomial programming problem, and subsequently, this resulting problem is linearized to produce a tighter relaxation in a higher dimensional space.\n\n## Comparative Strength\n\nRLT is a well-known strengthening technique for binary mixed-integer optimization that is known to dominate lift-and-project strengthening, which is based on disjunctive programming for single-variable disjunctions.\n\nThe search results focus primarily on theoretical optimization aspects rather than specific applications to 2D rectangle placement and packing problems. The references to packing appear in the context of general combinatorial optimization problems that can be solved using these techniques.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"8aa84a10-69e5-4bb0-a2ff-69e350ea2574","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:06:32.206Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9fe8c8ba-e849-441a-93d4-90f9c65e4a82","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
